    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity CAGR is not usable as the growth anchor: book equity was cut to 1.4bn by the FY2022 accelerated repurchase and has rebuilt since, so the 3-year equity CAGR (29.6%) diverges far more than 30% from revenue (9.0%) and EPS (14.9%). Growth is anchored on through-cycle revenue instead.",
    "notes": "FCF CAGRs are on the owner-FCF series (reported FCF less SBC less RSU tax withholding; no interest strip, net debt throughout). Owner FCF: FY2021 1,785m, FY2023 3,062m, FY2026 3,252m. The 3-year owner-FCF CAGR (2.0%) is below reported (4.2%) and far below EPS (14.9%): SBC plus withholding rose from 266m to 515m, and FY2026 working capital absorbed over 1bn. FY2023 to FY2026 also spans a full down-and-up cycle."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "FY2027 is a cyclical peak, not a new base. Backlog of 12.57bn and a 4.7bn Q2 guide carry revenue to ~18.3bn (+35%), and FY2028 adds a little more as 2nm, HBM and advanced-packaging ramps finish. Leading-edge customers then digest: revenue falls 10% in FY2029 and China keeps shrinking as a share under export controls. From the trough KLA grows 4-8% a year, slightly ahead of wafer fab equipment because process-control intensity rises with each node and services (23% of revenue, +16%) compound on the installed base. Revenue reaches ~26.8bn in FY2036, a 7% ten-year CAGR. EBITDA margin before SBC peaks at 49.5%, dips to 46.5% in the digestion and settles at 47.5%.",
    "bull": "AI capex proves durable rather than a bulge. Leading-edge logic, HBM and advanced packaging keep pulling process-control spend ahead of wafer fab equipment, KLA adds to its ~58% process-control share, and there is a pause in FY2029 rather than a contraction. Revenue compounds ~11% a year to ~38bn by FY2036 with EBITDA margin before SBC holding just above 50% as services scale and DRAM-component and tariff cost pressure fades. China stabilises at legacy-node demand and the Affiliates Rule is not reinstated in a harsher form.",
    "bear": "The fear behind the November 2026 semicap sell-off is right. Hyperscaler and AI capex is cut in calendar 2027, TSMC and the memory makers push out tools, and backlog (which KLA itself says does not indicate revenue timing) is rescheduled or cancelled. In parallel the Affiliates Rule returns and China, 30% of revenue, steps down again. Revenue falls 25% from the FY2027 peak over two years, EBITDA margin before SBC drops to 42.5% on under-absorption and persistent DRAM-component and tariff costs, and the recovery is a slow 3-6%. FY2036 revenue of ~18bn is no higher than the FY2027 peak."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "KLA is a dominant, very high-margin franchise valued here on what an owner collects through a full cycle, not on peak-year earnings. SBC and RSU tax withholding are charged as cash costs, working capital is charged on growth, and FY2027 is treated as a cyclical peak. The price is compared with that through-cycle value at a 10.5% required return.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Cycle timing: the base case puts a 10% revenue decline in FY2029. If AI-driven capex stays elevated longer the base is too low; if the cut comes in FY2028 it is too high.",
      "Discount rate: with the 10-year UST at 5.15% the model uses 10.5%. Each point of WACC moves base value by roughly 12-16%; see the sensitivity grid and required-return table.",
      "Terminal margin: 47.5% EBITDA before SBC is above any full-cycle average KLA has yet reported at smaller scale.",
      "SBC basis: RSU tax withholding (205m in FY2026, 239m in Q1 FY2027 alone) is summed with SBC expense; it scales with the share price and would fall if the stock does.",
      "Export controls on China (30% of FY2026 revenue) and TSMC concentration.",
      "Working-capital capture of -30% is calibrated on FY2025-FY2026; inventory builds may partly unwind in a downturn, which the engine credits symmetrically."
    ]
  },
